WEBVTT

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.760
This is Izakaya Nobu.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.490
It's an ordinary izakaya, but 
there is one extraordinary thing.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.990
<b>Nobuyuki Yazawa (32)
 Owner of the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.600
Our front door opens into 
a parallel universe!

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.000
<b>Shinobu Senke (23)
 Waitress at the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Isekai Izakaya

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Japanese Food From Another World

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.010
<b>Ale from the Ancient City</b>

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.500
<i>Nobu was accused by the 
Backeshof Trading Company</i>

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.450
<i>of selling a prohibited good, namely lager.</i>

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.620
<i>The survival of the store was at risk,</i>

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.670
<i>but the ones who saved us were our regulars.</i>

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.840
<i>After getting out of that pinch, we had been</i>

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.310
<i>finding our way back to a peaceful everyday.</i>

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:52.920
<i>However...</i>

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.040
What is this?

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.540
You are... Baron Brentano.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.810
<b>Brentano (41)
Baron of the Empire</b>

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.800
Indeed. It's been a while, Fräulein.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.620
My apologies for back then.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:16.680
No, no. I'm the one who needs to apologize.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:21.190
<b>Holger (50) 
Blacksmith Guild Meister. 
Member of the city council</b>

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.200
This scent... This is ale!

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.750
Correct.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.650
A-Ale? You mean this is all ale?

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:26.970
That's not all.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.060
There's also our prized product, 
Apfelwein, a beverage made from apples.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.570
Everyone! Tonight both fish 
and drinks are on me.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.470
Eat and drink as much as you like!

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.570
That's okay, right, Taishou?

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:45.370
Wait a minute. Why are you 
doing this, Baron Brentano?

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.580
This is to make up for the things
the servant who used to work for me did.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.550
Servant? You mean Damian-san.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.800
I heard that he started working 
for Backeshof after I fired him,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.240
and that he caused you trouble.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.990
What happened to him, anyway?

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.600
Backeshof is still being investigated, 
but Damian has been released.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:08.610
What?

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:13.830
All he did was suggest buying 
this place to Backeshof.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.920
There were no real serious 
charges against him.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.880
And thus he was cast out of Aitheria.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.860
<b>Gernot (37)
 Tax Collector
Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.520
Actually, the reason we were 
able to arrest Backeshof was

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.060
because we had the aid of Baron Brentano.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.350
You were helping us, Baron Brentano?

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.950
I did it for the good of Aitheria.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.800
Of Aitheria?

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.300
Backeshof was a man
surrounded by bad rumors.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:49.310
I used this opportunity to have 
Gernot-kun investigate him.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:53.820
The result is that we were able to 
defeat a villain with this case.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.460
As an influential person in Aitheria, 
it behooves me to thank you.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.750
Then I accept.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.430
Well then, I won't hold back!

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.440
<b>Ale from the Ancient City</b>

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.440
<b>Specially selected by Baron 
Brentano! Just give it a try!</b>

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.770
<b>Edwin (70) 
Deacon of the 
Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.100
Oh, my.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.070
To be able to behold such 
an ale before I die.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.970
<b>Lorenz (50)
Glass Craft Guild Meister. 
Member of the City Council.</b>

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.990
This smells really nice.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:25.620
<b>Hans (23)
Palace Guard
Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:25.620
<b>Berthold (32)
 Company Commander of 
Aitheria Palace Guards aka "the Devil"</b>

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:25.620
<b>Nikolaus (24)
Palace Guard
Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:25.610
I swear this is better than the 
Königsbräu from where I come from!

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:29.300
This is nothing like the imitations 
you find all over the place.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.620
It's the best of the best among 
ales, good enough to rival lager.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.390
As residents of this region, 
you should enjoy it fully.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:37.760
Prosit!

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.260
Prosit!

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.130
<b>Fizz</b>

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:44.810
It's so good!

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.470
It's so good I have to laugh!

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.000
Each barrel has a vaguely different taste.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.810
This barrel's better 
because it has more body.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.230
No, this one has more body.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.610
As always, your tongue is just useless.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.020
What did you say?!

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.530
Come now, both are tasty.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.650
You, too.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.050
You're the one who played 
the main role in this.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.700
Don't hold back! Drink up, drink up!

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.450
I only did my work.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.480
Nothing I did was for this pla—

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.690
Don't be like that!

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:20.110
How about you two?

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.720
Us, too?

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.200
All right. Tonight's special.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.820
Thank you.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.820
Thank you very much.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.920
Have you tried any ale in Aitheria before?

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.210
A couple of times.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.180
It seems that it didn't 
impress you too much.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.770
I can't blame you though.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.600
Lately Aitheria has been inundated 
with inferior products.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.310
But I'd really like you 
to get to know good ale.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.090
Go ahead. Have some.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:49.380
I will.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:53.380
This is...

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.020
<i>A sweet scent.</i>

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.730
<i>It reminds me of bananas.</i>

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.300
It's delicious.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.510
This is real ale?

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.530
<b>Eva (14)
Dishwasher at Nobu.
Resident of Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.380
You guys are unfair!

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.750
I think it's a little too soon for you.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.820
Eva-chan, there's some fresh 
apple juice over there, too.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:23.030
<b>Hermina (16)
Waitress at Nobu.
Berthold's wife</b>

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.270
Let's go have some together.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:23.040
Okay!

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.260
Tonight you should drink as much as you can!

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.820
If you're done with the ale, you 
can switch to Apfelwein as well!

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.920
It's so good you've not 
even dreamed about it!

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.470
Three cheers for Baron Brentano!

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.820
Long live Baron Brentano!

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:37.910
Prosit!

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.980
See, this is all because of me!

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:50.330
<b>Izakaya Nobu</b>

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:57.970
It's finally over.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:01.260
In the end, we only got that 
first glass in the beginning.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:05.910
But was it all right not to tell people 
that today was a special day?

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.680
This sort of thing is kind of embarrassing,

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.770
so it's better to celebrate quietly.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.180
You're right.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:17.760
It's been exactly half a 
year since Nobu opened up.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.450
Shinobu-chan, I'll make something.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.640
I've saved some ingredients.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.120
Really? That sounds great!

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.880
The ale was good, too. But...

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.130
For us, whatsontap is our mainstay!

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:35.680
Cheers!

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:41.880
This is the starter.

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.350
<b>Three Types of Sashimi</b>

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.350
<b>Nobu's recommended three types!</b>

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.340
Tuna, yellowtail, and octopus.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:47.900
That's such a pretty plating.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.910
Then here I go.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:58.870
It's richer than usual!

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.250
Yes. Until now, I had bought 
cuts that were good for

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.910
a light eating experience,
like I used to in the restaurant.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:06.590
But I thought this might be better.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:10.370
Yes, I think this flavor is more 
considerate of our customers' palates.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:12.230
You're good with words.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.250
Taishou, I'd like to eat some tempura.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:18.230
I figured you'd say that, 
so it's already prepared.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:19.890
As always, Taishou amazes.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.510
If nothing else, I know what you like.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.760
<b>Sizzle</b>

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:31.060
<b>Sizzle Sizzle Sizzle Sizzle</b>

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:35.830
<b>Tempura Assortment</b>

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:35.830
<b>Tempura tastes the best freshly 
fried and extra crispy!</b>

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:32.280
Here you go.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.840
Kisu, maitake mushroom, edible 
chrysanthemum, and squid.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:38.180
Oh, there's less batter.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.330
It's because we got better 
ingredients this time.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.830
And you prefer this over 
the flashy look, right?

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:46.420
I guess you knew that about me, too.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:50.550
<b>Crunch</b>

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.830
It's so nice and fresh.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:01.230
It's really highlighting the ingredients.

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:05.020
But you know, when I had that ale earlier,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:08.390
I thought that maybe we should 
change our dishes a little, too.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:09.540
You're right.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:12.870
I immediately remembered the dishes 
I had in the Ancient City.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:16.590
For both food and drink, there are 
things that come from that region.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.820
Maybe a restaurant needs 
to adjust to those things.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.270
You may be right.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.940
Thinking back this past
six months, everybody ate a lot

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.280
of things that they had never seen before.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.200
Hans-san got obsessed with oden.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.050
Berthold-san with kara-age.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:36.660
There was also kisu tempura 
and bonito tataki, too.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:34.060
<b>Crunch</b>

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:39.970
The eel native to Aitheria 
was a huge success.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:42.130
Everybody was eating that 
with a hearty appetite.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.330
Nikolaus-san was being saved by the tonjiru.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.160
And there was also someone

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.770
who fell in love with reishu, 
shiokara, and shutou.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:49.240
<b>Droop</b>

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:53.140
But I didn't think you'd serve 
a meal for the employees.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:51.460
<b>Wash</b>

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.430
W-Well, that's...

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.420
Though it's almost like 
that saved our place here.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:08:59.830
See?

00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.420
<b>Crunch</b>

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:04.820
Could that be...

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:06.900
Here you go. The last 
dish to tie it together.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:09.820
This is it! Salmon ochazuke!

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.810
<b>Salmon Ochazuke</b>

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.810
<b>This is <i>the</i> final dish to tie it together!</b>

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:12.160
Thank you!

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:27.700
So good!

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.570
Eating this really has a calming effect.

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:31.350
It does.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.220
You know, Taishou...

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.080
There's something I've been thinking 
about for a long time now.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:40.130
About what Björn-san said.

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:41.040
Right.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:44.150
You need to meet certain 
criteria for a good bar.

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:47.280
That's probably like what we had today.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.780
No, not just today.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:51.010
The other day, too.

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:53.830
And I think it's been this 
way for a long time now.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:56.300
If there is a bar that 
cares about its customers,

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:58.920
there will be customers 
who care about the bar.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.550
I can't phrase it eloquently,

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.000
but I think that's what he meant.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:09.990
You might be right.

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:11.480
Hey, Nobuyuki-san.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.980
Let's keep running Izakaya Nobu.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.490
Yeah, let's.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.340
Good.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:27.470
<b>Izakaya Nobu</b>

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.290
Next up, we've got Nobu Plus.

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.020
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.030
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:39.550
<b>Ginza</b>

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:36.190
This is a war zone!

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:37.630
We've come to Ginza.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:39.550
There are just so many food places here.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:43.020
Every time, there are so 
many things shown in Nobu.

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:46.270
<b>Kenichi Nagira</b>

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:44.890
You thought that we'd run out of material?

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:46.270
Who are you kidding?!

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.080
There are more than enough 
places to showcase.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:48.850
So without further ado...

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:49.880
You can look forward to this one!

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:51.360
I'm torn about where to go...

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:53.680
Cheap place... A cheap place...

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:54.830
A cheap and tasty place!

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:59.330
<b>Narrator
 Teiyu Ichiryusai</b>

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.810
Kenichi Nagira is going to go around the bars

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:01.510
here to find the amazing 
dishes featured in "Nobu."

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:01.820
<b>Kenichi Nagira's search for the "Nobu" bar</b>

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:03.840
Here we are.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.540
<b>Ginza
 Sashimi</b>

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.540
This is the izakaya I recommend.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:09.060
<b>Kenichi Nagira
 Born in Tokyo's Ginza. Made his debut in 1970 as a folk singer.
 Since then, he's been active as an actor, celebrity, essayist.
 Has been in multiple shows that take place in bars or Shitamachi</b>

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.610
The Ginza Bokujin not only has the 
kitchen chef's famous Japanese dishes,

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:15.400
<b>Ginza Bokujin</b>

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:15.430
but also a hundred kinds of 
local and national sake!

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.460
<b>Bokujin
 Owner/Chef    Kimura Takahisa-san</b>

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:16.710
You've got a nice place here.

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:17.460
Thank you.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:20.690
Sir, I'd like you to just 
bring out what you recommend.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:21.030
Certainly.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:24.840
I'll wait while drinking that 
brown stuff that foams.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:26.690
Whatsontap!

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.360
What a splendid fish!

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:33.340
And that focused look 
on his face is also cool!

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:35.820
We were just watching his 
graceful knife movements,

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.340
and it's already coming out!

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:38.580
Excuse me.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:39.340
See?

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.310
Oh, wow.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:42.990
It's three types of sashimi 
using fresh fish from Aomori.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:43.440
Beautiful!

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:44.350
You only use fish from there?

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:47.380
Yes. It's because I come from there.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:48.400
Oh, I see.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:51.250
I actually source the ingredients 
for all our dishes from Aomori.

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.020
That's impressive.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:54.620
Did you train somewhere?

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:58.040
I did train for about ten 
years at Kitcho.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.260
The place for Japanese food!

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:00.040
I probably need to
 save up for this.

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:01.870
Ah, yes.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:03.740
Maybe I shouldn't eat this then...

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:06.610
You're right. 
This stuff is high quality!

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:07.770
All right, I'll have some.

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:08.800
Please go ahead.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.100
What are you starting with?
What is that?

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:12.470
What kind of fish is this?

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:13.910
That's a kanpachi or greater yellowtail.

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:14.620
Ah, kanpachi.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:15.360
How's the kanpachi from Aomori?!

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:16.430
It's got this resistance to it.

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:21.120
Really soft is good, too, but sometimes 
you need something you can bite.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:21.420
You said it!

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:22.750
This is mebaru.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:24.260
Mebaru, dark rockfish.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:27.000
That looks so enticing.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:31.750
This one has an even more 
notable consistency.

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:33.820
This is grilled gindara 
in the Saikyouyaki style.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:35.200
Wow, that's so pretty.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.180
It looks really great.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.950
You're right. Wait, when 
did you order that sake?!

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:41.720
You wet your mouth with the sake first.

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:44.780
And then you eat 
that marbled sablefish...

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:45.800
This is great.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:47.000
Of course it is!

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.630
We pickle them in miso for three days,

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.560
then let them rest for one more.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:53.670
These considerations towards the 
food happen in a place where we

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.270
can't see them, but they are 
definitely apparent in the flavor.

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:58.060
This is brilliantly delicious.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:59.110
Thank you very much.

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:00.540
That's the skill of a true master!

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:03.260
What's this stuff that looks like the stuff
in packing material from online shopping?

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:05.260
That's a seaweed
 called puchimarine.

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:07.230
It's seaweed? Aquamarine?

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:08.540
It's not a jewel!

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:08.980
Puchimarine.

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:09.560
I see.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:13.360
So, how is it? Tell us!

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:20.560
You know, at first I thought 
it looked like plastic.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:21.820
But this is actually quite good.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:25.530
Even Nagira-san approves
of "Ginza Bokujin."

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.480
It's a one minute walk
from Ginza Station.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:29.090
<b>Assistance: Gurunabi</b>

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:31.780
If you want have good fish and 
sake, come here, come here!
